WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.881
hello-devops already counts the requests it serves.

2
00:00:05.881 --> 00:00:11.183
It keeps the numbers in memory, and shows them on a plain web page, its metrics page.

3
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.399
Each line is a metric name, then labels in braces, then the value right now.

4
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.000
As requests come in, the number grows.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.779
But the page only shows the value right now.

6
00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:24.684
Nothing remembers what it was ten minutes ago.

7
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:31.264
Prometheus is a server that collects these numbers every few seconds, and stores them with a timestamp.

8
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:32.942
It collects by pulling.

9
00:00:32.942 --> 00:00:34.996
On a timer, it fetches the page.

10
00:00:34.996 --> 00:00:39.295
One fetch is called a scrape, and the thing it scrapes is a target.

11
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.003
The lab scrapes every fifteen seconds.

12
00:00:42.003 --> 00:00:43.716
The default is one minute.

13
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:46.482
Look at the metric hello_responses_total.

14
00:00:46.482 --> 00:00:49.280
One name, but three different sets of labels.

15
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.134
Each label set is its own time series.

16
00:00:52.134 --> 00:00:56.916
A time series is the metric name, plus one exact set of label values.

17
00:00:57.133 --> 00:01:00.606
Change any label value, and you have a different series.

18
00:01:00.606 --> 00:01:02.032
Here, that makes three.

19
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:09.579
Prometheus adds two labels of its own: job, the job name from its config, and instance, the target's address.

20
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.165
Now watch a scrape.

21
00:01:11.165 --> 00:01:15.979
Each one adds one sample to every series: a timestamp, and a value.

22
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.113
Fifteen seconds later, the counter has grown.

23
00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:21.120
Another scrape, another sample.

24
00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:25.820
And again, every fifteen seconds, for as long as Prometheus runs.

25
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:29.680
Prometheus keeps only what the counter read at each scrape.

26
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.947
Never the single requests in between.

27
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:37.931
A graph of one series is just these samples, as points, joined by a line.

28
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:41.196
This metric is a counter.

29
00:01:41.196 --> 00:01:42.909
A counter only goes up.

30
00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:46.124
Or it drops to zero, when the process restarts.

31
00:01:46.124 --> 00:01:48.224
Then it starts counting up again.

32
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:53.856
So the raw value, like 303, only says how many since the process started.

33
00:01:53.856 --> 00:01:56.233
On its own, it tells you little.

34
00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:59.715
The useful number is how fast it grows.

35
00:01:59.715 --> 00:02:02.576
Here it grew by 204 in 45 seconds.

36
00:02:02.576 --> 00:02:05.605
That is about 4.5 requests a second.

37
00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:06.995
That is a rate.

38
00:02:06.995 --> 00:02:13.113
The PromQL function rate() works it out for every series, even across restarts.

39
00:02:13.113 --> 00:02:14.429
Day 51 shows how.

40
00:02:15.433 --> 00:02:17.134
The app shows its numbers.

41
00:02:17.134 --> 00:02:22.562
Prometheus pulls them on a timer, and stores one sample per series at every scrape.

42
00:02:22.562 --> 00:02:25.309
For a counter, you read how fast it grows.
